## Context

M1 needs a reproducible end-to-end acceptance gate that does not depend on a public website.

## Scope

- add an end-to-end test using a controlled local HTTP server;
- serve a sufficiently large body in multiple chunks;
- execute the download through the application manager;
- verify persisted lifecycle ordering and progress;
- verify partial-file and finalization behavior;
- reopen SQLite and reload the completed job;
- cover unknown content length, remote rejection, interrupted response, and
  cancellation;
- update README capability and limitation statements.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A multi-chunk resource is downloaded without collecting the full body.
- [ ] Final bytes exactly match the server fixture.
- [ ] No final output is visible before successful finalization.
- [ ] The completed job survives repository reopen.
- [ ] Unknown `Content-Length` downloads complete correctly.
- [ ] Remote rejection produces `Failed` and no final output.
- [ ] Interrupted response produces `Failed` and preserves partial output.
- [ ] Cancellation produces `Cancelled` and no final output.
- [ ] Tests require no external network service.
- [ ] README describes the working single-stream HTTP capability honestly.
- [ ] Workspace formatting, tests, Clippy, and cross-platform CI pass.

## Out of scope

- proving restart resume;
- performance benchmarking;
- segmented HTTP;
- authenticated browser downloads.

Depends on: #12  - #20

## Context

The first HTTP milestone should be exercisable through an actual application client. The desktop layer must remain a thin composition and presentation layer over Gunda's application API.

## Scope

- introduce the initial Tauri and Svelte desktop application;
- compose `DownloadManager`, `SqliteDownloadRepository`, and the HTTP engine;
- open the application database from an appropriate local application-data path;
- provide a minimal form for URL, destination directory, and optional filename;
- create and start a direct download;
- display lifecycle state and byte progress;
- allow cancellation of an active download;
- load persisted jobs when the application starts;
- keep Tauri and Svelte dependencies outside `gunda-core`.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A user can submit a valid direct HTTP URL.
- [ ] The job is persisted before transfer starts.
- [ ] State and progress changes appear in the desktop client.
- [ ] The user can cancel an active transfer.
- [ ] Completed output appears only after finalization.
- [ ] Previously persisted jobs are visible after reopening the application.
- [ ] Invalid input and failures produce safe actionable messages.
- [ ] No request header values or private paths are logged by the UI bridge.
- [ ] Closing a window does not leave mutable application state borrowed by UI code.
- [ ] The desktop layer contains no HTTP transfer or SQL implementation.

## Out of scope

- polished visual design;
- system tray;
- background daemon;
- multiple simultaneous downloads;
- settings and history management;
- browser integration.

Depends on: #19

## Context

The application manager must coordinate persistence, HTTP inspection, transfer, progress, filesystem finalization, and runtime events while preserving lifecycle ordering.

## Scope

- allow the manager to execute a queued direct HTTP job;
- coordinate the initial HTTP implementation without freezing a premature
  multi-protocol engine registry;
- persist each lifecycle transition before exposing it;
- record inspection metadata and resolved destination;
- start the streamed transfer;
- process confirmed progress checkpoints;
- enter `Finalizing` before committing output;
- enter `Completed` only after final output commit succeeds;
- translate engine and filesystem failures into safe `DownloadFailure`;
- clean up active task ownership after every outcome.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] The successful path follows:
      `Queued -> Inspecting -> Downloading -> Finalizing -> Completed`.
- [ ] Every state is persisted before its runtime event is emitted.
- [ ] `Completed` is impossible before finalization succeeds.
- [ ] Transfer failure follows a valid transition to `Failed`.
- [ ] Failure details contain no URLs, headers, response bodies, or paths.
- [ ] The manager is the only component mutating and persisting the aggregate.
- [ ] The HTTP engine never calls the repository directly.
- [ ] Only one active transfer is required; general queue scheduling remains deferred.
- [ ] Completed jobs can be reloaded after reopening SQLite.
- [ ] Tests verify both successful and failed lifecycle ordering.

## Out of scope

- generalized engine registration;
- HLS and DASH selection;
- multiple concurrent jobs;
- priorities;
- retry and recovery.

Depends on: #12 - #18

## Context

M1 requires active direct HTTP transfers to respond to user cancellation without producing a completed output or misclassifying cancellation as a network failure.

## Scope

- connect `DownloadCommand::Cancel` to an active transfer;
- provide a cancellation signal owned by the application layer;
- stop response consumption and file writes promptly;
- wait for transfer activity to stop before committing `Cancelled`;
- persist the cancelled state before emitting its event;
- preserve the partial file for explicit later removal;
- handle races between cancellation, transfer completion, and failure;
- remove active worker bookkeeping after termination.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Cancelling an active transfer stops further writes.
- [ ] Cancellation does not create the final destination.
- [ ] Cancellation is not recorded as `Failed`.
- [ ] `Cancelled` is persisted before the state-change event is emitted.
- [ ] The partial output remains available for explicit cleanup.
- [ ] A cancellation request cannot commit after successful finalization.
- [ ] Repeated or late cancellation does not corrupt manager state.
- [ ] Active worker state is cleaned up after cancellation.
- [ ] Tests cancel a deliberately slow local response deterministically.
- [ ] Tests contain no fixed long sleeps.

## Out of scope

- pause and resume;
- restart recovery;
- resumable cancelled jobs;
- bulk cancellation.

Depends on: #16 and #17

## Context

Progress must be visible during transfer and durable enough to represent a checkpoint. Persisting every network chunk would generate unnecessary SQLite writes, while emitting uncommitted progress would make runtime state disagree with durable state.

## Scope

- define the initial checkpoint policy by elapsed time and/or transferred bytes;
- accept progress reports from the HTTP engine;
- validate progress through `DownloadProgress`;
- persist checkpoints through the repository;
- update manager state only after persistence succeeds;
- emit `ProgressChanged` only for confirmed state;
- always persist the final transfer byte count;
- support unknown total length;
- avoid treating speed and ETA as durable fields.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Progress is based on bytes successfully written to partial output.
- [ ] Progress never exceeds a known total.
- [ ] SQLite is not updated for every small network chunk.
- [ ] The final byte count is persisted before finalization begins.
- [ ] Events are emitted only after the corresponding checkpoint is committed.
- [ ] Repository failure does not produce a successful progress event.
- [ ] Unknown totals do not produce an invented percentage.
- [ ] Restarting the repository preserves the last committed checkpoint.
- [ ] Tests use deterministic checkpoint control without timing-dependent sleeps.
- [ ] Speed and ETA are not stored in SQLite.

## Out of scope

- recovery from checkpoint/filesystem disagreement;
- progress aggregation across multiple jobs;
- bandwidth metrics;
- HLS segment progress.

Depends on: #12 and #16

## Context

The initial HTTP engine must transfer ordinary resources without buffering the complete response in memory.

## Scope

- implement the direct single-stream HTTP transfer path;
- consume response-body chunks incrementally;
- write each chunk completely to the partial file;
- count progress only after bytes have been accepted by the file writer;
- support responses with known and unknown length;
- classify network, remote response, and filesystem failures;
- return protocol-neutral inspection, progress, completion, and failure outcomes
  to the application layer;
- keep persistence and lifecycle mutation outside the HTTP engine.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Large test responses are streamed incrementally.
- [ ] The implementation never calls an API that collects the entire body.
- [ ] Every response chunk is written with complete-write semantics.
- [ ] Downloaded bytes represent successfully written bytes.
- [ ] Known and unknown response lengths are supported.
- [ ] Early connection closure produces a failure and no final output.
- [ ] Non-success HTTP responses do not become completed files.
- [ ] The engine does not access SQLite or mutate `DownloadJob`.
- [ ] Tests use local responses split into many chunks.
- [ ] The resulting partial file matches the served bytes exactly.

## Out of scope

- retry;
- resume;
- Range requests;
- parallel connections;
- bandwidth limiting.

Depends on #13 and #15

## Context

Incomplete network data must never be written directly over the final destination. Gunda needs an explicit staging lifecycle before the first transfer engine can safely produce files.

## Scope

- derive a job-owned partial path from the resolved destination;
- create partial files without following accidental destination collisions;
- keep staging output in the final destination filesystem when possible;
- flush and synchronize completed output before finalization;
- commit completed output through an atomic rename where supported;
- apply conflict policy immediately before the final commit;
- leave the final destination untouched after failed transfers;
- translate filesystem failures into safe failure categories;
- keep staging paths outside the generic `ResolvedDestination` domain type.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Transfer data is written only to a partial path.
- [ ] Different jobs cannot accidentally share the same partial file.
- [ ] A failed transfer does not create or replace the final file.
- [ ] Successful finalization moves the complete partial output to the final path.
- [ ] Overwrite happens only under the explicit overwrite policy.
- [ ] Rename and fail conflict policies behave deterministically.
- [ ] Permission, existing-file, and missing-directory failures are classified.
- [ ] Tests use temporary directories.
- [ ] Platform-specific finalization behavior passes Linux and Windows CI.
- [ ] Partial and final paths are never included in user-safe error messages.

## Out of scope

- startup recovery;
- cross-filesystem copy fallback;
- cleanup retention policies;
- resuming partial files.

Depends on: #14

## Context

Before transferring a direct resource, Gunda must classify it, record available metadata, and resolve a safe final destination. Remote URL paths and filenames are untrusted input.

## Scope

- inspect the HTTP response before streaming its body;
- validate successful response status;
- read `Content-Length` when present;
- read `Content-Type` when present;
- classify the resource as a direct file;
- select a filename from explicit destination intent or a safe URL-path fallback;
- sanitize remote-derived filenames;
- prevent absolute paths and directory traversal;
- apply the selected file conflict policy;
- produce and persist `ResourceDescriptor` and `ResolvedDestination`.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Known `Content-Length` becomes the initial total byte count.
- [ ] Missing `Content-Length` leaves total progress unknown.
- [ ] `Content-Type` is preserved as optional generic resource metadata.
- [ ] A preferred filename takes precedence when valid.
- [ ] Remote path components cannot escape the selected directory.
- [ ] Empty, reserved, or unusable names receive a safe fallback.
- [ ] Conflict policy never overwrites an existing file unless explicitly allowed.
- [ ] Resource and destination metadata are persisted before downloading begins.
- [ ] Tests cover malicious names, encoded traversal, missing names, and conflicts.
- [ ] Unix and Windows filename behavior is tested where platform-specific.

## Out of scope

- full `Content-Disposition` support;
- remote filename encoding heuristics;
- redirects requiring request-policy decisions;
- media playlist detection.

Depends on: #12 and #13

## Context

Direct HTTP downloads and future streaming engines need a shared low-level HTTP transport. The transport must expose HTTP behavior without implementing download lifecycle, filesystem, scheduling, or persistence policy.

## Scope

- introduce the `gunda-http` crate;
- configure an asynchronous HTTP client with TLS support;
- translate `RequestContext` into HTTP requests;
- preserve explicitly supplied public request headers;
- expose response status, final URL, headers, and streaming body data;
- configure bounded connection and request behavior;
- map transport failures into safe application-facing categories;
- create controlled local HTTP server fixtures for tests;
- keep HTTP client types outside `gunda-core`.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A direct HTTP/HTTPS GET can be initiated through the transport.
- [ ] Response bodies are exposed as a stream rather than collected into memory.
- [ ] Public request headers reach the local test server correctly.
- [ ] Response status and headers are available before body consumption.
- [ ] Transport errors do not expose headers, credentials, or response bodies.
- [ ] Tests use only a controlled local server.
- [ ] No live public website is required by the test suite.
- [ ] `gunda-core` does not depend on the selected HTTP client library.
- [ ] The transport does not update jobs or write files.
- [ ] Linux and Windows CI pass.

## Out of scope

- retries;
- byte ranges;
- segmented transfers;
- authentication persistence;
- proxy configuration;
- HLS parsing.

## Context

The repository currently persists and restores only newly created queued jobs. The first HTTP download requires durable lifecycle transitions, inspection metadata, resolved destinations, progress checkpoints, and failures.

## Scope

- extend the generic SQLite schema through a new ordered migration;
- persist resolved destinations;
- persist generic resource metadata;
- persist lifecycle state and progress updates;
- persist safe failure information;
- restore jobs in every valid lifecycle state;
- introduce a controlled aggregate restoration API;
- keep protocol-specific recovery data outside the generic downloads table;
- ensure the application manager remains the only lifecycle writer.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A new migration adds the required generic aggregate fields.
- [ ] Existing databases migrate without losing queued jobs.
- [ ] Every `DownloadState` has an explicit stable database representation.
- [ ] Resource kind, display name, content type, resolved destination, progress,
      failure, and timestamps round-trip through SQLite.
- [ ] Invalid enum values and inconsistent nullable fields are rejected as
      `InvalidData`.
- [ ] Byte conversions between SQLite `i64` and domain `u64` are checked.
- [ ] Repository writes are transactional.
- [ ] A failed write is not reflected in manager state or runtime events.
- [ ] Tests cover migration, persistence, reopen, and corrupted rows.
- [ ] Formatting, tests, and Clippy pass with warnings denied.

## Out of scope

- HTTP validators and range-resume metadata;
- HLS segment state;
- startup reconciliation with partial files;
- retry policy.

## Context

Contributor documentation still describes the repository as containing a single Rust binary and refers to core and storage as proposed components. The repository now contains a workspace with implemented `gunda-core` and `gunda-storage` crates.

The documented development workflow must match the actual repository before M0 is considered complete.

## Scope

- update the repository layout in `CONTRIBUTING.md`;
- distinguish implemented components from planned components;
- document the workspace-wide validation commands;
- describe the current SQLite test expectations;
- verify that README and architecture status descriptions agree;
- remove stale statements that describe existing crates as nonexistent.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `CONTRIBUTING.md` accurately describes `gunda-core` and `gunda-storage`.
- [ ] Local commands use `--workspace` where appropriate.
- [ ] Contributor guidance does not claim that an executable downloader exists.
- [ ] Planned HTTP, desktop, browser, daemon, and CLI components remain clearly
      identified as future work.
- [ ] Documentation agrees with the repository structure.
- [ ] All documented validation commands execute successfully.

## Out of scope

- publishing a permanent roadmap document;
- documenting HTTP APIs that do not exist;
- user installation instructions;
- release and packaging documentation.

## Context

Gunda needs structured diagnostics for application and persistence operations, but request URLs, destination paths, browser context, and header values may contain private or sensitive information.

Library crates must emit structured events without configuring a global tracing subscriber or leaking user-provided values.

## Scope

- add `tracing` as a workspace dependency;
- instrument repository and manager operations with structured spans;
- use stable safe fields such as operation, download ID, state, and error kind;
- keep subscriber initialization outside library crates;
- preserve the distinction between diagnostic information and user-facing
  errors;
- review existing `Debug` implementations and error mapping for accidental
  sensitive-data exposure;
- add tests or a test subscriber proving sensitive values are not emitted.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Library crates do not initialize a global tracing subscriber.
- [ ] Repository open, migration, create, list, and lookup operations have
      structured diagnostic context.
- [ ] Manager startup and creation operations have structured diagnostic context.
- [ ] Events may include `download_id`, operation, state, and safe error kind.
- [ ] Events do not include source URLs, header values, browser page URLs,
      destination paths, SQL parameters, or database paths.
- [ ] Sensitive headers cannot appear in captured test output.
- [ ] User-facing repository errors remain safe to display.
- [ ] Tracing does not change domain or repository behavior.
- [ ] Formatting, tests, and Clippy pass with warnings denied.

## Out of scope

- log file rotation;
- telemetry export;
- OpenTelemetry;
- metrics dashboards;
- desktop logging configuration;
- logging protocol response bodies.

## Context

Gunda can persist and reload an individual queued download when its `DownloadId` is already known. The application layer still has no way to load the complete persistent queue during startup or coordinate newly created jobs.

The initial `DownloadManager` should establish this application boundary before protocol engines and network transfers are introduced.

## Scope

- extend `DownloadRepository` with an operation for loading persisted jobs;
- return jobs in a deterministic order;
- introduce the initial `DownloadManager` in `gunda-core`;
- load persisted jobs before the manager becomes available to clients;
- keep an application-level registry indexed by `DownloadId`;
- create jobs through the repository before exposing them to the manager;
- provide immutable lookup and snapshot/list operations;
- produce a `Created` notification only after persistence succeeds;
- propagate startup and repository failures without exposing partially loaded
  application state;
- implement SQLite and application-layer tests.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] An empty database starts an empty manager successfully.
- [ ] Multiple queued jobs are loaded after closing and reopening SQLite.
- [ ] Loaded jobs preserve their IDs and creation order.
- [ ] A newly created job is persisted before becoming visible through the manager.
- [ ] A failed repository creation does not add a job to manager state.
- [ ] Failure while loading startup state prevents partially initialized state
      from being exposed.
- [ ] Duplicate persisted IDs are treated as invalid repository data.
- [ ] The manager does not depend on SQLx, SQLite, Tokio, Tauri, or HTTP types.
- [ ] Tests use an application-layer fake repository where appropriate.
- [ ] An integration test proves startup loading through
      `SqliteDownloadRepository`.
- [ ] Formatting, tests, and Clippy pass with warnings denied.

## Out of scope

- network transfers;
- protocol engine selection;
- scheduling and concurrency;
- lifecycle updates beyond initial creation;
- pause, resume, retry, and cancellation execution;
- startup recovery of active transfers.

- When a user clicks the "Reserve rocket" button, action needs to be dispatched to update the store. You need to get the ID of the reserved rocket and update the state. Remember you mustn't mutate the state. Instead, you need to return a new state object with all rockets, but the selected rocket will have an extra key `reserved` with its value set to `true`. You could use a JS `filter()` or `map()` to set the value of the new state - i.e.:
```javascript
const newState = state.map(rocket => {
    if(rocket.id !== id) 
        return rocket;
    return { ...rocket, reserved: true };
});

```

- Regardless of which method you choose, make sure you place all your logic in the reducer. In the React view file, you should only dispatch the action with the correct rocket ID as an argument.

- When a user clicks the "Reserve dragon" button, action needs to be dispatched to update the store. You need to get the ID of the reserved dragon and update the state. Remember you mustn't mutate the state. Instead, you need to return a new state object with all dragons, but the selected rocket will have an extra key `reserved` with its value set to `true`. You could use a JS `filter()` or `map()` to set the value of the new state - i.e.:
```javascript
const newState = state.map(rocket => {
    if(dragon.id !== id) 
        return dragon;
    return { ...dragon, reserved: true };
});

```

- Regardless of which method you choose, make sure you place all your logic in the reducer. In the React view file, you should only dispatch the action with the correct dragon ID as an argument.

- When a user clicks the "Join Mission" button, action needs to be dispatched to update the store. You need to get the ID of the selected mission and update the state. Remember you mustn't mutate the state. Instead, you need to return a new state object with all missions, but the selected mission will have an extra key `reserved` with its value set to `true`. You could use a JS `filter()` or `map()` to set the value of the new state - i.e.:
```javascript
const newState = state.map(rocket => {
    if(mission.id !== id) 
        return mission;
    return { ...mission, reserved: true };
});

```

- Regardless of which method you choose, make sure you place all your logic in the reducer. In the React view file, you should only dispatch the action with the correct rocket ID as an argument.

Missions that the user has joined already should show a badge "Active Member" instead of the default "NOT A MEMBER" and a button "Leave Mission" instead of the "Join Mission" button (as per design).


Use the React conditional rendering syntax:
```javascript
{rocket.reserved && ( 
    // render Cancel Rocket button
)}
```

